description:
Director of Resource Management and Planning (Academic Affairs Director)

**POSITION DETAILS**

Founded in 1971 in Long Island City, Queens **,** LaGuardia Community College is one of seven community colleges of the City University of New York (CUNY). LaGuardia offers more than sixty associate degree programs and more than fifty workforce training, ESOL, GED, and pre-college programs. In 2023, LaGuardia served approximately 24,000 students. More than one-third of LaGuardia’s degree-seeking students are born outside the United States; they come from 136 countries and speak 43 heritage languages. Sixty percent are first-generation college students.

Virtually all LaGuardia students are ethnic minorities (89 percent), 58 percent are women, 27 percent are over the age of 25. Forty-six percent of associate degree students are Hispanic. Upon graduation most students transfer to four-year colleges, typically in CUNY, to complete their baccalaureate degrees. Graduates of career programs such as Nursing, Computer Technology, and Veterinary Technology enter the workforce. LaGuardia ranked fifth among U.S. community colleges in economic mobility – moving low-income students into the middle class and beyond – in studies by Stanford University (2017) and the Brookings Institution (2020). Please visit www.laguardia.edu to learn more.

Reporting to the Provost & Senior Vice President, the Director of Academic Resource Management & Planning serves as a senior member of the Provost's leadership team and provides strategic leadership for institutional resource development, divisional planning, financial management, and cross-functional initiatives across the Division of Academic Affairs.

Collaborating closely with the Provost, AA leadership, department chairs, faculty, and administrative leaders, the Director oversees grant stewardship and supports the development of innovative initiatives that advance the College's strategic priorities, transforming institutional needs into competitive funding proposals. The Director also coordinates divisional financial planning and management for Tax Levy and grant-funded projects.

Focused on complex projects that strengthen academic excellence, workforce development, student success, and operational effectiveness, the Director serves as a trusted advisor to the Provost by ensuring the effective stewardship of financial and institutional resources, translating institutional priorities into actionable strategies, and cultivating strategic partnerships. The Director also helps to plan and coordinate grant-funded divisional initiatives.Responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following:

* Lead the conceptual development of competitive grant proposals that advance institutional priorities and Academic Affairs strategic goals;
* Collaborate with faculty, department chairs, administrators, institutional leaders, and external partners to identify funding opportunities and transform innovative ideas into fundable projects;
* Coordinate proposal development across academic departments while collaborating closely with Finance, Sponsored Programs, Institutional Research, and external partners;
* Guide proposal development from concept through completion, including project design, program narratives, partnership cultivation, work plans, evaluation strategies, implementation frameworks, and budget development;
* Ensure that proposed initiatives align with the College's strategic plan, institutional priorities, available resources, and sponsor requirements;
* Supervise the work of pre- and post-award grant support staff in Academic Affairs;
* Monitor and report on divisional operating budgets, including tax levy, grant, and other funding sources;
* Collaborate with Academic Affairs budget analysts to develop financial projections, monitor expenditures, reconcile accounts, and ensure sound fiscal stewardship;
* Track divisional financial performance and identify budget trends, risks, and opportunities;
* Prepare executive reports, dashboards, and financial presentations for the Provost and senior leadership;
* Coordinate budget planning associated with new initiatives, staffing requests, capital investments, and strategic priorities;
* Conduct institutional, organizational, and departmental needs assessments to identify opportunities for innovation and continuous improvement;
* Partner with department chairs and Academic Affairs leadership to develop, implement, and evaluate strategic projects across the division;
* Coordinate complex cross-functional initiatives involving Academic Affairs, Finance, Institutional Research, Student Affairs, Information Technology, Human Resources, Sponsored Programs, and Administrative Services;
* Prepare executive briefing materials, reports, presentations, and recommendations for senior leadership;
* Represent the College at meetings, conferences, and public events as assigned by the Provost.

**QUALIFICATIONS**

Bachelor's Degree and eight years' relevant experience required.

OTHER QUALIFICATIONS

* Minimum of eight years of progressively responsible experience in higher education administration, Academic Affairs, strategic planning, grants and sponsored programs, budgeting, project management, or institutional administration.
* Demonstrated success leading the development and implementation of competitive grant proposals for federal, state, local, corporate, and private funding agencies, including creating budgets, auditing and managing programs, and leading and managing complex school-wide initiatives and organizational change;
* Experience conceptualizing innovative programs and translating institutional priorities into fundable initiatives;
* Experience collaborating with budget analysts, department chairs, faculty, senior administrators, and external partners.
* Strong analytical skills with the ability to interpret financial and operational data and develop strategic recommendations.

**Company Profile:**



Morgan Stanley is a leading global financial services firm providing a wide range of investment banking, securities, investment, and wealth management services. The Firm's employees serve clients worldwide, including corporations, governments, and individuals from more than 1,200 offices in 43 countries.



In the Research division, we offer timely, integrated analysis of companies, sectors, markets, and economies, helping clients with their most critical decisions.



**What you’ll do in the role:**



Morgan Stanley & Co. LLC is seeking an Associate, Research in New York, New York to Provide financial analysis reports and materials for individual industries and sectors-specific companies. Analyze industry data to develop an industry-specific vision. Update and create financial models. Analyze emerging trends in an industry or macro-economy. Assess relative stock valuations and review findings with team as well as internal and external clients. Build relationships with global colleagues, salespeople, traders and external investing clients. Combine quantitative processes with fundamental insights to identify industry key performance indicator, develop sector-specific signals, build conviction on a call, predict stock returns, and track company specific risks. Develop, back-test, and implement statistics models, machine learning, and Al to test the efficacy of sector data. Explore and evaluate alternative data to gain insights into new alpha and develop trading strategies. Build tools to query, clean, analyze raw data through databases, and work closely with technology team to ensure data quality and delivery. Publish leading research insight notes and present at industry conferences. Actively source new ideas and collaborate with other research teams. Collaborate with sales and marketing, providing quant proof statements to both internal and external clients. Telecommuting permitted up to 1 day per week.



**What you’ll bring to the role:**

* Requires a Master’s in Computational Finance, Financial Engineering, or a related field of study.

* Requires any amount of experience with the following skills:

* Deep Neural Networks;
* Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM);
* Convolutional Neural Networks;
* Tree-based Methods including Decision Trees and Random Forest;
* Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and Dimensionality reduction;
* Lasso Regression, Ridge Regression, and Logistic Regression;
* Classification including k-Nearest Neighbor;
* Time-series Modelling including Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA)-based methods;
* Financial Modelling, Statistical Analysis, and Big Data Analysis;
* Forecasting and Probability Theory;
* Data Set Evaluation;
* Back-testing;
* Geospatial Analysis;
* Python, Git, Linux, Q/KDB, and SQL;
* MS Office Suite;
* Investment Report Writing;
* Microeconomics and Macroeconomics; and
* Stochastic Calculus

**WHAT YOU CAN EXPECT FROM MORGAN STANLEY:**

At Morgan Stanley, we raise, manage and allocate capital for our clients – helping them reach their goals. We do it in a way that’s differentiated – and we’ve done that for 90 years. Our values - putting clients first, doing the right thing, leading with exceptional ideas, committing to diversity and inclusion, and giving back - aren’t just beliefs, they guide the decisions we make every day to do what's best for our clients, communities and more than 80,000 employees in 1,200 offices across 42 countries. At Morgan Stanley, you’ll find an opportunity to work alongside the best and the brightest, in an environment where you are supported and empowered. Our teams are relentless collaborators and creative thinkers, fueled by their diverse backgrounds and experiences. We are proud to support our employees and their families at every point along their work-life journey, offering some of the most attractive and comprehensive employee benefits and perks in the industry. There’s also ample opportunity to move about the business for those who show passion and grit in their work.

To learn more about our offices across the globe, please copy and paste https://www.morganstanley.com/about-us/global-offices into your browser.

Expected base pay rates for the role will be between $200,000 and $200,000 per year at the commencement of employment. However, base pay if hired will be determined on an individualized basis and is only part of the total compensation package, which, depending on the position, may also include commission earnings, incentive compensation, discretionary bonuses, other short and long-term incentive packages, and other Morgan Stanley sponsored benefit programs.

Morgan Stanley is an equal opportunity employer committed to building and maintaining a workforce that is diverse in experience and background. Our recruiting efforts reflect our strong commitment to a culture of inclusion, where individuals are hired, developed, and advanced based on their skills and talents.

Our workforce reflects a broad cross-section of the global communities in which we operate, bringing a variety of backgrounds, talents, perspectives, and experiences.

For more information, please visit : https://www.morganstanley.com/people-opportunities/eeo .

**Company Profile:**

Morgan Stanley is a leading global financial services firm providing a wide range of investment banking, securities, investment, and wealth management services. The Firm's employees serve clients worldwide, including corporations, governments, and individuals from more than 1,200 offices in 43 countries.



In the Fixed Income division, we work in fast-paced and constantly changing global markets to assess and manage risk, trade securities, manage relationships with clients, and structure and execute innovative transactions.



**What you’ll do in the role:**

Morgan Stanley & Co. LLC is seeking an Associate, Banking in New York, New York to provide investment banking services to the largest and most notable issuers nationwide in the public sector, housing, not-for-profit healthcare, and tax-exempt structured finance groups. Prepare and execute live transactions, including crafting rating agency, investor roadshow, and sales memorandum materials. Prepare product pitches based on market dynamics and responses to requests for proposal. Perform cash flow analyses to evaluate debt financing alternatives. Update credit models and conduct peer credit analysis. Monitor debt portfolios, identifying opportunities for refinancing or restructuring. Conduct debt capacity and affordability analysis. Research and analyze market data to share insights with clients. Field and respond to client inquiries, including travel for client meetings and conferences.



**What you’ll bring to the role:**

* Requires a Bachelor's degree in Finance, Economics, or a related field of study

* Requires two (2) years of experience in the position offered or two (2) years as an Analyst, or a closely related occupation

* Requires two (2) years of experience with the following skills:

* Excel, DBC Finance, Word and PowerPoint;
* industry resources including EMMA (Electronic Municipal Market Access), IPREO, and SDC Platinum;
* performing refunding analyses in DBC Finance and structuring new money transactions;
* refunding alternatives including a current refunding, forward refunding, and tender refinancing of tax-exempt and eligible taxable debt;
* reading and interpreting issuer financial statements and documents including Official Statements, Capital Improvement Plans, Annual Financial Reports, and EMMA notices;
* creating comprehensive debt profiles for issuers and evaluating potential refunding opportunities;
* determining when an issuer should utilize fixed rate products versus variable rate products;
* drafting Responses to Proposals (RFPs);
* preparing meeting books and updates by summarizing analytical information into client-ready PowerPoints and PDFs;
* collaborating with the underwriting desk for scale requests, reviewing wires, setting up market update and pricing calls, and coordinating pricing call materials during live deals;
* collaborating with financial advisors and quantitative teams to structure deals and run cashflows;
* reviewing documents including Preliminary Official Statements (POS), Official Statements (OS), and Bond Purchase Agreements (BPA);
* preparing ratings presentations and investor presentations;
* pricing processes including securing SLGS subscription, wiring good faith checks, verifying insurance requirements, and sending cashflows to verification agent; and
* closing processes and ensuring compliance with all MSRB (Municipal Securities Rulemaking Board) regulatory requirements including G-11 wires, G-32 filing, preparing closing memos, SDC Platinum credit confirmation, and post-pricing books.

**WHAT YOU CAN EXPECT FROM MORGAN STANLEY:**

At Morgan Stanley, we raise, manage and allocate capital for our clients – helping them reach their goals. We do it in a way that’s differentiated – and we’ve done that for 90 years. Our values - putting clients first, doing the right thing, leading with exceptional ideas, committing to diversity and inclusion, and giving back - aren’t just beliefs, they guide the decisions we make every day to do what's best for our clients, communities and more than 80,000 employees in 1,200 offices across 42 countries. At Morgan Stanley, you’ll find an opportunity to work alongside the best and the brightest, in an environment where you are supported and empowered. Our teams are relentless collaborators and creative thinkers, fueled by their diverse backgrounds and experiences. We are proud to support our employees and their families at every point along their work-life journey, offering some of the most attractive and comprehensive employee benefits and perks in the industry. There’s also ample opportunity to move about the business for those who show passion and grit in their work.

